1
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:04,370
Oh. Mm.

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:05,620
Hi.

3
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:05,620
Hey.

4
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:08,170
You have got to try this cheesecake.

5
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:10,420
Oh, you know, I'm not that much of a sweet tooth...

6
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:14,630
Oh, my God, it's so creamy.

7
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:14,630
Oh.

8
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:18,350
Oh, my God. That's the best cheesecake I've ever had.

9
00:00:18,510 --> 00:00:19,890
Where did you get this?

10
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:24,520
It was at the front door when I got home. Somebody sent it to us.

11
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,810
Chandler, this isn't addressed to you.

12
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:30,070
This is addressed to Mrs. Braverman downstairs.

13
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:32,440
Thief.

14
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:36,490
I didn't read the box before I opened it...

15
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,700
...and you can't return a box after you opened it.

16
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,950
Why not?

17
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,950
Because it's too delicious.

18
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:46,330
Chandler, you stole this cheesecake. That is wrong.

19
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:48,290
No, no, no, it is going to be okay...

20
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:51,250
...because Mrs. Braverman is gonna send away for a free one.

21
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,050
And that way, we all win.

22
00:00:53,210 --> 00:00:57,430
The only losers are the big cheesecake conglomerate:

23
00:00:57,590 --> 00:01:00,010
"Mama's Little Bakery."

24
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:02,720
I feel terrible.

25
00:01:02,890 --> 00:01:05,230
I'm a horrible, horrible, horrible person.

26
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:08,440
Well, I'm sorry, what?

27
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,860
I should get going. Big day at work.

28
00:01:52,190 --> 00:01:54,190
You know how I'm in a coma?

29
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:58,320
Today they do a test on me and it turns out I'm not brain-dead.

30
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:00,450
So...

31
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:02,200
Ah-ah, Mr. Smarty-Pants.

32
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:04,830
It's just my character that's not brain-dead.

33
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:07,830
Pheebs, still on for tonight?

34
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,620
Absolutely. Okay.

35
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:10,620
All right, I'll see you at 8.

36
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:12,170
Oh, what's at 8?

37
00:02:12,340 --> 00:02:13,920
Oh, I have dinner plans with Joey.

38
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:17,170
We get together about once a month to discuss the rest of you guys.

39
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:20,760
Wow. Did not know that.

40
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:23,470
May I say how lovely you look today?

41
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,220
Duly noted.

42
00:02:25,930 --> 00:02:27,560
Oh, so for tomorrow...

43
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,190
...do you want to rent a car and drive down together or what?

44
00:02:30,770 --> 00:02:31,850
What are you talking about?

45
00:02:32,230 --> 00:02:34,020
Cousin Frannie's wedding. It's tomorrow night.

46
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:37,480
You were invited?

47
00:02:38,570 --> 00:02:40,530
No.

48
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:42,780
Oh, my God, I can't believe this.

49
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:45,280
I knew Mom and Dad were invited. I thought that was it.

50
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:49,710
I mean, from the ages of 7 to 9, Frannie and I were inseparable.

51
00:02:49,870 --> 00:02:53,710
Well, maybe since the age of 9, Frannie's made some new friends.

52
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:58,760
Look, it's probably just a mistake. Let me call Aunt Cheryl, okay?

53
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:02,090
Maybe you are invited and the invitation just got lost in the mail.

54
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:05,470
Yeah, well, you call her and tell her that, you know, when we were kids...

55
00:03:05,640 --> 00:03:09,020
...her precious little Frannie tried to undress me several times, okay?

56
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:12,560
If I hadn't stopped her, there probably wouldn't be a wedding to go to.

57
00:03:13,230 --> 00:03:15,860
She tried to undress me too.

58
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:19,070
I used to undress my cousin Glenn.

59
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:30,370
Joseph Francis Tribbiani, are you home yet?

60
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:32,580
Um, I think he's still out. What's wrong?

61
00:03:32,750 --> 00:03:34,370
Well, I'll tell you, Rachel Karen Green.

62
00:03:35,380 --> 00:03:38,340
I had plans with Joey tonight, and he left me this note.

63
00:03:39,090 --> 00:03:42,260
"Pheebs, can't make it. Got a date. Talk to you later.

64
00:03:42,430 --> 00:03:44,130
Big Daddy."

65
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:48,060
"Big Daddy"?

66
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:48,060
Oh, that's a nickname we were trying out.

67
00:03:48,220 --> 00:03:52,520
Hey, you know what nickname never caught on? "The Ross-a-tron."

68
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,190
Hey.

69
00:03:55,400 --> 00:03:58,190
Oh, here's Joseph Francis.

70
00:03:58,360 --> 00:04:01,900
Whoa, what are you middle-naming me for?

71
00:04:02,070 --> 00:04:03,400
I left you a note.

72
00:04:03,570 --> 00:04:05,950
So what? That doesn't give you the right to ditch me.

73
00:04:06,120 --> 00:04:10,330
Hey, you can cancel plans with friends if there's a possibility for sex.

74
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:12,290
Phoebe, he's right. That is the rule.

75
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:13,750
I don't accept this rule.

76
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,330
When we make plans, I expect you to show up.

77
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:19,750
Okay, I can't just be a way to kill time till you meet someone better.

78
00:04:19,920 --> 00:04:24,630
You know, boyfriends and girlfriends are gonna come and go, but this is for life.

79
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:28,970
Wow. I'm so sorry. I had no idea it would bother you this much.

80
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:30,510
Well, it does.

81
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:33,230
Okay, can I make it up to you? Huh?

82
00:04:33,390 --> 00:04:34,640
I'm sorry.

83
00:04:34,810 --> 00:04:38,440
How about dinner tomorrow night? I'll pay for myself.

84
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:42,940
Okay, you wore me down.

85
00:04:44,650 --> 00:04:48,780
Joe, while you're over there, how about another beer for "The Ross-a-tron"?

86
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:51,950
"Ross..." Is that back?

87
00:04:57,290 --> 00:04:59,710
Hi.

88
00:04:57,290 --> 00:04:59,710
The other cheesecake came.

89
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:02,130
They delivered it to the wrong address again.

90
00:05:03,130 --> 00:05:05,970
So just bring it back downstairs. What's the problem?

91
00:05:06,130 --> 00:05:08,590
I can't seem to say goodbye.

92
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:12,010
Are you serious? Chandler, we ate an entire cheesecake two days ago...

93
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:13,220
...and you want more?

94
00:05:13,390 --> 00:05:16,100
Well, I've forgotten what it tastes like, okay?

95
00:05:16,270 --> 00:05:19,230
It was cheesecake. It was fine.

96
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:23,530
It had a buttery, crumbly, graham-cracker crust...

97
00:05:23,690 --> 00:05:26,070
...with a very rich...

98
00:05:26,240 --> 00:05:30,370
...yet light cream-cheese filling.

99
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:35,870
Wow, my whole mouth just filled with saliva. Ahem.

100
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:39,120
Know what? Forget it. We are just hungry.

101
00:05:39,290 --> 00:05:41,840
We have not had lunch. We are just lightheaded.

102
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:45,590
So let us go out and have lunch and forget about the cheesecake.

103
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:47,880
We'll drop it off downstairs so we're not tempted.

104
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:49,470
Where do you wanna go to lunch?

105
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:51,300
Mama's Little Bakery, Chicago, Illinois.

106
00:05:55,180 --> 00:05:56,640
Aw.

107
00:05:56,810 --> 00:05:59,190
Mel Tormé died.

108
00:05:59,350 --> 00:06:01,810
Joey, that paper's like a year old.

109
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:05,440
Oh, does that mean the Sam Goody's sale is over?

110
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:08,570
Hey.

111
00:06:07,450 --> 00:06:08,570
Hey.

112
00:06:08,740 --> 00:06:11,740
So I finally heard back from Aunt Cheryl...

113
00:06:11,910 --> 00:06:14,330
...and apparently it wasn't a mistake.

114
00:06:14,490 --> 00:06:16,540
Uh, there's limited seating in the hall...

115
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:21,580
Limited seating? I am just one tiny person.

116
00:06:22,250 --> 00:06:24,380
Yeah, but she doesn't know that.

117
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,210
I mean, the last time she saw you...

118
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:31,260
...you would've turned one of those little wedding chairs into kindling.

119
00:06:33,180 --> 00:06:36,470
"Limited seating." That's such a lame excuse.

120
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,730
That's not the reason why she's not inviting me.

121
00:06:38,890 --> 00:06:42,980
What's the big deal? I wasn't even invited to the ceremony, just the reception.

122
00:06:43,150 --> 00:06:45,190
And you know what? If it makes you feel any better...

123
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:47,320
...Joan and I will just make an appearance...

124
00:06:47,490 --> 00:06:50,820
...and then we'll leave early as a sign of protest.

125
00:06:51,490 --> 00:06:53,570
Joan?

126
00:06:51,490 --> 00:06:53,570
Yeah, Joan Tedeschi, my date.

127
00:06:53,740 --> 00:06:56,280
She's an assistant professor in the Linguistics Department.

128
00:06:56,450 --> 00:06:57,790
Tall, very beautiful.

129
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:02,330
And despite what some people say, not broad-backed.

130
00:07:03,290 --> 00:07:06,670
Wait a minute. You got "Ross Geller and guest"?

131
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:08,960
I wasn't invited, and you got "and guest"?

132
00:07:09,170 --> 00:07:12,800
Uh, excuse me, I do have to interrupt on Ross' behalf.

133
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:14,850
I think the rule applies here.

134
00:07:15,010 --> 00:07:16,970
Since he has a chance to get on broad-back...

135
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:18,390
Not broad-back!

136
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:22,520
Wait a minute. You're bringing me.

137
00:07:22,690 --> 00:07:24,350
What? I can't cancel on Joan.

138
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:26,770
Why not?

139
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:26,770
Did you not hear me?

140
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:30,610
She's an assistant professor in the Linguistics Department, okay?

141
00:07:30,780 --> 00:07:32,700
They're wild.

142
00:07:33,410 --> 00:07:36,780
Why do you want to come anyway?

143
00:07:33,410 --> 00:07:36,780
Because...

144
00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:38,660
...she's my cousin.

145
00:07:38,830 --> 00:07:41,830
I mean, we grew up together.

146
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,540
Family, you know? And all that's important to me.

147
00:07:46,250 --> 00:07:49,340
Okay, all right. I'll take you.

148
00:07:49,510 --> 00:07:50,710
I'll go call Joan.

149
00:07:51,970 --> 00:07:53,680
Oh, that's nice.

150
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:55,680
Family should be there.

151
00:07:56,050 --> 00:07:58,100
This is her wedding. Happiest day of her life.

152
00:07:58,260 --> 00:07:59,890
We'll see. Ha, ha.

153
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:06,900
Well, thank you for lunch.

154
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:09,070
Wait a minute, I didn't pay. I thought you paid.

155
00:08:09,230 --> 00:08:11,900
So apparently, we just don't pay for food anymore.

156
00:08:14,910 --> 00:08:17,820
Do you see what I see?

157
00:08:14,910 --> 00:08:17,820
Huh! It's still there.

158
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:21,080
Mrs. Braverman must be out.

159
00:08:24,750 --> 00:08:27,790
She could be out of town. Maybe she'll be gone for months.

160
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:29,840
By then, the cheesecake may have gone bad.

161
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:31,920
We don't want her to come back to bad cheesecake.

162
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:33,760
That could kill her.

163
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:33,760
Wouldn't want that.

164
00:08:33,930 --> 00:08:35,010
No, we're protecting her.

165
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:36,680
We should take it.

166
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:36,680
But move quick.

167
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:38,760
Why?

168
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:38,760
I heard her moving around in there.

169
00:08:38,930 --> 00:08:40,310
Go, go, go.

170
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:47,230
Oh, my God, that's David.

171
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:49,400
Who?

172
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:49,400
David, the scientist guy.

173
00:08:49,570 --> 00:08:51,070
David that I was in love with.

174
00:08:51,230 --> 00:08:54,320
David who went to Russia and broke my heart, David.

175
00:08:54,610 --> 00:08:55,700
Oh, my God.

176
00:08:55,860 --> 00:08:58,070
Oh, you say someone's name enough, they turn around.

177
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,700
Phoebe.

178
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,700
David.

179
00:09:06,330 --> 00:09:09,460
What are you doing here? Aren't you supposed to be in Russia?

180
00:09:09,630 --> 00:09:12,000
Yeah, I'm just in town for a conference.

181
00:09:12,170 --> 00:09:15,300
Um, God, you look phenomenal.

182
00:09:15,470 --> 00:09:16,970
Well, ha, ha...

183
00:09:17,130 --> 00:09:18,470
Yeah.

184
00:09:19,010 --> 00:09:22,010
You look great too. Did you get a haircut?

185
00:09:22,310 --> 00:09:24,770
Yeah. Well, I got like 30 of them.

186
00:09:24,930 --> 00:09:26,270
Yeah.

187
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,690
Um, look, I got a confession to make.

188
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:28,690
Uh-huh.

189
00:09:28,850 --> 00:09:31,230
Um, I was hoping to run into you here.

190
00:09:31,400 --> 00:09:34,940
I didn't know that I should call or not. You know, I was only in town a few days...

191
00:09:35,110 --> 00:09:37,860
...and, you know, I didn't want to intrude on your life or anything.

192
00:09:38,030 --> 00:09:40,030
But I really wanted to see you...

193
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:43,080
...but I didn't know if you wanted to see me.

194
00:09:43,950 --> 00:09:48,920
Of course I would want to see you. I think about you all the time.

195
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:51,330
Really? Because I think about you all the time.

196
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,670
Really?

197
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:53,670
There's a statue in Minsk...

198
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:55,880
...that reminds me of you so much.

199
00:09:56,050 --> 00:09:58,340
I mean, um, it's actually of Lenin...

200
00:09:59,340 --> 00:10:02,140
...but, you know, at certain angles... PHOEBE: Yeah.

201
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:05,310
Anyway, do you want to have dinner tonight?

202
00:10:05,470 --> 00:10:07,060
Yes.

203
00:10:07,230 --> 00:10:09,850
Oh, no.

204
00:10:07,230 --> 00:10:09,850
Oh.

205
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:11,310
Uh, what?

206
00:10:10,020 --> 00:10:11,310
I can't.

207
00:10:11,480 --> 00:10:14,770
I can't believe I have plans. I can't. Can you do it tomorrow?

208
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:19,360
Uh, no, I have to go in a few hours. I have to be on the redeye.

209
00:10:19,860 --> 00:10:22,660
Well, listen, you know, next time you're in Minsk, uh...

210
00:10:22,830 --> 00:10:26,580
Phoebe? Can I talk to you for a second?

211
00:10:22,830 --> 00:10:26,580
Uh-huh.

212
00:10:26,750 --> 00:10:27,790
What are you doing?

213
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:31,630
Well, I have plans with Joey tonight.

214
00:10:27,960 --> 00:10:31,630
So? He'll understand.

215
00:10:31,790 --> 00:10:34,130
No, he won't. And that's not even the point.

216
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:39,590
Monica, I made a whole speech about how you do not cancel plans with friends.

217
00:10:40,260 --> 00:10:44,050
And now, you know, what? Just because, potentially, the love of my life...

218
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:49,100
...comes back from Russia just for one night, I should change my beliefs?

219
00:10:49,270 --> 00:10:51,270
I should change my beliefs.

220
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:56,480
No. No, no. If I don't have my principles, I don't have anything.

221
00:10:56,650 --> 00:10:57,980
God, you are so strong.

222
00:10:58,150 --> 00:11:02,030
Or I should rush through dinner with Joey and I could meet David at 9.

223
00:11:09,250 --> 00:11:11,500
Oh, my God, that is so good.

224
00:11:11,750 --> 00:11:15,250
I'm full. And yet I know if I stop eating this...

225
00:11:15,420 --> 00:11:17,250
...I'll regret it.

226
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:20,800
Hey, what have you got there?

227
00:11:21,220 --> 00:11:22,590
Oh, it's, um...

228
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:25,550
It's tofu cake. Do you want some?

229
00:11:22,760 --> 00:11:25,550
Aah.

230
00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:31,060
What are you doing tonight?

231
00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:31,060
Huh?

232
00:11:31,230 --> 00:11:33,520
Uh.

233
00:11:31,230 --> 00:11:33,520
Dude. Dude.

234
00:11:34,730 --> 00:11:38,070
Sorry. Uh, I got those plans with Phoebe. Why?

235
00:11:38,230 --> 00:11:41,150
Oh, really? Uh, Monica said she had a date at 9.

236
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:44,030
What? Tonight?

237
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:44,030
That's what Monica said.

238
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:45,910
After she gave me that big speech...

239
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:49,160
...she goes and makes a date on the same night she has plans with me?

240
00:11:49,330 --> 00:11:51,830
I think she's trying to pull a fast one on Big Daddy.

241
00:11:58,210 --> 00:12:01,460
Here, I think this is us. Yeah.

242
00:12:01,630 --> 00:12:04,130
Limited seating, my ass.

243
00:12:04,300 --> 00:12:06,680
Let's just see who made the cut.

244
00:12:06,850 --> 00:12:09,600
Hi. Hi. I'm Monica Geller.

245
00:12:09,770 --> 00:12:12,270
How do you know the bride and groom?

246
00:12:09,770 --> 00:12:12,270
I worked with Frannie.

247
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:14,980
Used to work with her. Used to.

248
00:12:15,150 --> 00:12:17,400
I'm a relative and I didn't get invited, okay?

249
00:12:17,570 --> 00:12:19,940
A blood relative. Blood.

250
00:12:21,690 --> 00:12:24,490
Stop saying "blood" to strangers.

251
00:12:25,320 --> 00:12:28,240
So how about you? How do you know the happy couple?

252
00:12:28,410 --> 00:12:31,290
We went to college with both of them. And now we live next door.

253
00:12:32,750 --> 00:12:34,120
Okay, you're fine.

254
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:41,130
Hey. Are you guys ready?

255
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:41,130
Hey. Yes.

256
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:45,130
I will have a green salad, um, house dressing and water's fine.

257
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:47,390
Okay.

258
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:47,390
And for you, sir?

259
00:12:47,550 --> 00:12:50,390
Yeah. This slow-roasted salmon...

260
00:12:50,560 --> 00:12:51,970
...just how slow are we talking?

261
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:55,270
It's already been roasted.

262
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:59,310
Oh, then no. Maybe I should hear those specials again.

263
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:02,320
Oh, Joey, we've heard the specials three times, okay?

264
00:13:02,490 --> 00:13:06,400
There's prime rib, mahi-mahi and a very special lobster ravioli.

265
00:13:06,570 --> 00:13:11,330
Actually, we're out of the lobster ravioli.

266
00:13:06,570 --> 00:13:11,330
Oh, well, that changes everything.

267
00:13:13,370 --> 00:13:14,580
You know what, Pheebs?

268
00:13:13,370 --> 00:13:14,580
Uh-huh.

269
00:13:14,750 --> 00:13:17,960
You were right before. Friends are so important.

270
00:13:18,130 --> 00:13:20,630
Yeah, I'm very wise. I know.

271
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:22,340
You know what I really want?

272
00:13:22,510 --> 00:13:25,720
What?

273
00:13:22,510 --> 00:13:25,720
Is to have a long, long talk.

274
00:13:25,880 --> 00:13:29,890
You know? Get Joey out on the open road and really open him up.

275
00:13:31,010 --> 00:13:32,600
Any progress?

276
00:13:31,010 --> 00:13:32,600
Yes.

277
00:13:33,470 --> 00:13:35,600
I will have the lobster ravioli.

278
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:38,900
God, Joey, this is taking forever.

279
00:13:39,230 --> 00:13:40,730
What's the rush?

280
00:13:39,230 --> 00:13:40,730
Well...

281
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:43,650
...I just have, you know, an appointment.

282
00:13:43,860 --> 00:13:45,740
And it's very important.

283
00:13:45,900 --> 00:13:47,070
What is it?

284
00:13:48,070 --> 00:13:49,780
Well...

285
00:13:49,950 --> 00:13:50,990
...it's a date.

286
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:54,870
A date? No, no, Pheebs, you must be mistaken...

287
00:13:55,040 --> 00:13:56,750
...because you wouldn't schedule a date...

288
00:13:56,910 --> 00:13:58,670
...on the night you have plans with a friend.

289
00:13:58,830 --> 00:14:00,710
Come on. Don't make me feel badly about this.

290
00:14:00,880 --> 00:14:02,420
No, I'm gonna.

291
00:14:03,960 --> 00:14:06,260
That's right. Yeah, you made me feel really guilty...

292
00:14:06,420 --> 00:14:07,760
...about going out with that girl.

293
00:14:07,930 --> 00:14:11,720
Like I did something terrible to you. Now, Pheebs, you're doing the same thing.

294
00:14:11,890 --> 00:14:14,600
That's not the same thing. This is totally different.

295
00:14:14,770 --> 00:14:17,850
This is with David. Remember David, the scientist guy?

296
00:14:18,020 --> 00:14:19,560
Okay, he's very special to me.

297
00:14:19,730 --> 00:14:21,900
Okay, well, my girl from the other night was special.

298
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:23,310
She was a scientist too.

299
00:14:23,730 --> 00:14:26,360
She was?

300
00:14:23,730 --> 00:14:26,360
Well, she graduated high school.

301
00:14:27,360 --> 00:14:28,900
Okay, whatever. Know what?

302
00:14:29,070 --> 00:14:30,410
I don't have time to convince you.

303
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:32,780
He's only here for four hours and I'm gonna go see him.

304
00:14:32,950 --> 00:14:35,740
Fine. Fine!

305
00:14:32,950 --> 00:14:35,740
Yeah. Yeah.

306
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,750
What are you still doing here? I told you, lobster ravioli.

307
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:44,460
Hey.

308
00:14:43,460 --> 00:14:44,460
Hey.

309
00:14:44,630 --> 00:14:47,590
Oh, I was just about to leave. I didn't think you were coming.

310
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:50,260
Oh, I wouldn't have missed this. Ha, ha.

311
00:14:50,430 --> 00:14:52,390
Well, I'm very glad you're here.

312
00:14:52,680 --> 00:14:55,220
Oh, you're such a gentleman.

313
00:14:55,390 --> 00:14:57,520
Come on, we're going to my place.

314
00:15:04,650 --> 00:15:06,650
Are you eating the cheesecake without me?

315
00:15:06,820 --> 00:15:08,480
Mm-mm.

316
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:12,950
I will give you $100 to whistle right now.

317
00:15:17,830 --> 00:15:19,580
How can you eat the cheesecake without me?

318
00:15:19,750 --> 00:15:20,790
What are you gonna do?

319
00:15:20,960 --> 00:15:24,710
Gonna run and tell Monica or Joey? No, you'd have to tell them what we did.

320
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:28,630
We are dessert stealers. We are living outside the law.

321
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:31,300
You know what? I don't trust you with this cake.

322
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:34,090
And I got it first, and I'm taking it back.

323
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:34,090
What?

324
00:15:34,260 --> 00:15:37,350
What? Oh, no, you don't.

325
00:15:34,260 --> 00:15:37,350
Oh, yes.

326
00:15:37,510 --> 00:15:39,850
You think I trust you with it?

327
00:15:40,020 --> 00:15:41,980
We're gonna split it. You take half, I take half.

328
00:15:42,140 --> 00:15:43,650
That's not fair. You had some.

329
00:15:43,810 --> 00:15:46,770
Oh, you know what? I think Monica would be very interested to know...

330
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:49,860
...that you called her cheesecake dry and mealy.

331
00:15:50,030 --> 00:15:52,650
What do we use to split it?

332
00:15:50,030 --> 00:15:52,650
Okay.

333
00:15:54,950 --> 00:15:57,740
All right, pick a half.

334
00:15:54,950 --> 00:15:57,740
Okay.

335
00:15:57,910 --> 00:16:01,040
Well, this side looks bigger.

336
00:15:57,910 --> 00:16:01,040
Mm-hm.

337
00:16:01,210 --> 00:16:04,960
Uh, but there's more crust on this side.

338
00:16:05,130 --> 00:16:07,380
Yeah. So maybe if I measure it...

339
00:16:07,540 --> 00:16:08,840
For God's sake, pick a piece.

340
00:16:09,050 --> 00:16:13,010
All right, I pick that one.

341
00:16:09,050 --> 00:16:13,010
So the smaller piece.

342
00:16:13,630 --> 00:16:15,590
Okay. There you go.

343
00:16:15,760 --> 00:16:18,930
Enjoy your half, my friend. But that is it.

344
00:16:19,100 --> 00:16:22,730
No sharing, no switching, and don't come crying to me...

345
00:16:22,890 --> 00:16:24,770
...if you eat your piece too fast.

346
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:33,900
Oh!

347
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:39,030
You gonna give me some of your piece?

348
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:39,030
Oh, no.

349
00:16:39,490 --> 00:16:42,750
No switching, no sharing. And don't come crying to me.

350
00:16:42,910 --> 00:16:46,830
Ha, ha. I may just sit here and have my cake all day.

351
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:50,210
Just sit here in the hallway and eat my...

352
00:16:58,890 --> 00:17:02,140
Never met a girl that could me feel...

353
00:17:02,310 --> 00:17:04,180
Ross, sweetheart.

354
00:17:04,350 --> 00:17:06,060
Oh, hey, Aunt Millie.

355
00:17:06,230 --> 00:17:10,310
Isn't it a beautiful wedding?

356
00:17:06,230 --> 00:17:10,310
Yes. Yes, it is.

357
00:17:14,740 --> 00:17:16,740
Every time, on the lips.

358
00:17:16,910 --> 00:17:18,990
Why? Why on the lips?

359
00:17:22,370 --> 00:17:24,950
There's Frannie. Won't she be happy to see me?

360
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:27,120
Now, wait a minute. You be nice, all right?

361
00:17:27,290 --> 00:17:29,540
I didn't bring you here so you could ambush her.

362
00:17:29,710 --> 00:17:32,920
Frannie was the one who found your Playboys and showed them to Mom.

363
00:17:33,090 --> 00:17:34,300
That bitch.

364
00:17:39,850 --> 00:17:42,100
Monica. What...?

365
00:17:42,260 --> 00:17:44,970
Am I doing here? Surprised to see me?

366
00:17:45,140 --> 00:17:47,480
Ross brought me. How do you like that?

367
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:49,980
Hi, Frannie. Congratulations.

368
00:17:51,860 --> 00:17:55,150
You invite my brother, you invite my whole family, and not me? Why?

369
00:17:55,320 --> 00:17:57,070
Why wouldn't you want me at your wedding?

370
00:17:57,240 --> 00:17:58,740
What could I have possibly done?

371
00:18:00,660 --> 00:18:02,240
Stuart.

372
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:05,160
I believe you know my husband.

373
00:18:06,580 --> 00:18:10,750
So it's really a question of "who" could you have possibly done.

374
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:21,800
I hate this, but I have to go.

375
00:18:19,680 --> 00:18:21,800
Mm-hm.

376
00:18:21,970 --> 00:18:24,600
I can't miss my flight.

377
00:18:24,770 --> 00:18:27,020
Are you sure? I bet there's another flight to Minsk in...

378
00:18:27,180 --> 00:18:28,390
July.

379
00:18:38,490 --> 00:18:40,910
That's really beautiful. What does it mean?

380
00:18:41,070 --> 00:18:43,700
"Please clean my beakers."

381
00:18:44,790 --> 00:18:46,200
I don't get out of the lab much.

382
00:18:49,370 --> 00:18:51,710
I thought it meant something else.

383
00:18:51,880 --> 00:18:56,050
Yeah, well, I really actually wanted to say, um...

384
00:18:56,210 --> 00:18:57,380
...that.

385
00:18:57,550 --> 00:19:00,010
But, um, I figured I probably shouldn't because...

386
00:19:00,180 --> 00:19:03,550
...you know, I have to leave.

387
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:06,930
Hmm. No, you're right. Don't say it.

388
00:19:08,270 --> 00:19:10,390
I do, though.

389
00:19:12,350 --> 00:19:14,060
I do too.

390
00:19:22,570 --> 00:19:24,240
Well, bye, Phoebe.

391
00:19:35,750 --> 00:19:39,300
Okay, now's not the time, Joey, all right? You can yell at me tomorrow.

392
00:19:39,460 --> 00:19:42,220
No, no. No, Pheebs. I'm not gonna yell at you.

393
00:19:43,090 --> 00:19:45,260
I just started thinking about you and David...

394
00:19:45,430 --> 00:19:49,430
...and I remember how bummed you were the first time he left.

395
00:19:51,770 --> 00:19:53,980
Oh, Pheebs, come here.

396
00:19:54,980 --> 00:19:58,230
Are you okay?

397
00:19:54,980 --> 00:19:58,230
No, I'm not okay.

398
00:19:58,690 --> 00:20:02,150
The only guy I've ever been crazy about is going to Minsk...

399
00:20:02,320 --> 00:20:05,320
...and I may never see him again.

400
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:07,330
Hey, you could always visit him.

401
00:20:07,490 --> 00:20:10,080
Oh, right. Like they're gonna let me have a passport.

402
00:20:11,540 --> 00:20:14,250
Anything I can do? Whatever you need.

403
00:20:16,290 --> 00:20:18,290
Well, now...

404
00:20:18,460 --> 00:20:23,050
...if you achieve positronic distillation of subatomic particles before he does...

405
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:24,590
...then he could come back.

406
00:20:31,390 --> 00:20:33,770
I could give it a shot.

407
00:20:39,480 --> 00:20:42,360
Oh, look. There's a piece that doesn't have floor on it.

408
00:20:42,530 --> 00:20:45,820
Stick to your side.

409
00:20:42,530 --> 00:20:45,820
Come on, now.

410
00:20:56,500 --> 00:20:59,090
All right, what are we having?

411
00:21:06,800 --> 00:21:08,680
Oh, wait, I forgot my wrap.

412
00:21:08,970 --> 00:21:11,310
Oh, okay, wait here.

413
00:21:28,490 --> 00:21:31,030
Hi, sweetie. Are you leaving?

414
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:34,540
Well...

415
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:34,540
Give us a kiss. Come on.

416
00:21:43,300 --> 00:21:46,380
Why? Why on the lips?

